\pard\qc\b\fs32 Preliminary Investigation and Mechanistic Synthesis: A Novel Quadriherbal Formulation Targeting AMPK Activation and PPAR-\u947? Downregulation for Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease (MASLD) and Diabetes\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qc {\b Mabrig Korie}\par
\pard\qc Independent Researcher, Natural Products & Traditional Knowledge Integration\par
\pard\qc Mabrig Korie Ministries Research Division | MABRIG Digital Media, Port Harcourt, Rivers State, Nigeria\par
\pard\qc contact@mabrigkorie.org\par
\pard\qc\fs18 Mabrig Journal of Interdisciplinary Research (MJIR), Vol. 2, Issue 1, 2026, pp. 136\u8211?158\par
\pard\qc\fs18 DOI: 10.5678/mjir.v2i1.007 \u8212? Article Type: Preliminary Investigation / Hypothesis-Generating Synthesis\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 Abstract\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) \u8212? the condition long known as NAFLD before the 2023 multisociety nomenclature revision (Rinella et al., 2023) \u8212? now affects an estimated quarter to a third of adults worldwide (Younossi et al., 2016, 2018), yet the therapeutic cupboard remains nearly bare: lifestyle change still does most of the work, and the pharmacological agents that have shown genuine promise, such as the GLP-1 receptor agonist semaglutide (Newsome et al., 2021), carry costs and access barriers that put them out of reach for most patients in low- and middle-income countries (Paik et al., 2020; Friedman, Neuschwander-Tetri, Rinella, & Sanyal, 2018). This preliminary investigation takes as its starting point a 2026 in vivo and in silico study by Sarkar and Das, which found that leaf extract of {\i Bambusa vulgaris} \u8212? common bamboo \u8212? substantially protected high-fat-diet rats against MASLD, principally by activating AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) and suppressing peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-\u947?) in liver and adipose tissue.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Read alongside the independent literature on {\i Mangifera indica} (mango) leaf, whose mangiferin-rich extracts stimulate GLP-1 secretion and exert antioxidant, hepatoprotective effects (Jariyapongskul et al., 2025; Nadella et al., 2016; Muruganandan, Srinivasan, Gupta, Gupta, & Lal, 2005), the bamboo findings suggested something nobody appeared to have written down yet: that these two plants engage genuinely complementary, non-overlapping arms of the same metabolic problem, and that a third and fourth African herb \u8212? chosen for compatible phytochemistry and a documented history of traditional use against liver and metabolic complaints (Sofowora, 1993; Iwu, 1993; Gurib-Fakim, 2006) \u8212? could plausibly be added without duplicating what the first two already do. This article performs that synthesis. No new animal or human experiments were conducted; instead, published mechanistic, phytochemical, and ethnopharmacological evidence is mapped onto a single proposed quadriherbal formulation (QHF), its predicted multi-target mechanism is laid out in full, and a staged roadmap toward the in vitro, in vivo, and eventual clinical work that alone could confirm or refute the hypothesis is set out explicitly.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 {\b Keywords:} MASLD; Bambusa vulgaris; Mangifera indica; quadriherbal formulation; AMPK; PPAR-\u947?; ethnopharmacology; hepatoprotective; antidiabetic; African traditional medicine; polyherbalism\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 1. Introduction: A Bamboo Leaf and an Old Idea\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Sometime in 2026, a research team led by Sarkar and Das published a finding that, on its face, looked like one more entry in the long catalogue of plant-extract-protects-rats-from-fatty-liver papers that crosses a hepatologist's desk every month. Rats fed a high-fat diet, as expected, became obese and developed the steatotic, inflamed liver tissue characteristic of metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD). Rats fed the same diet alongside daily doses of {\i Bambusa vulgaris} leaf extract did not \u8212? or at least did so far less. What made the paper worth a second look was not the protective effect itself, which polyphenol-rich plant extracts have demonstrated in rodent models many times before, but the specificity of the mechanism the authors were able to demonstrate: a clear increase in phosphorylated, activated AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) and a clear decrease in peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-\u947?) expression, in both liver and adipose tissue, accompanied by in silico docking data suggesting which of the extract's compounds were doing the work (Sarkar & Das, 2026).\par
\pard\qj\sa160 That degree of mechanistic resolution is unusual in the ethnopharmacological literature, where \u8220?hepatoprotective\u8221? is too often a label applied to anything that lowers a liver enzyme on a blood panel without much explanation of why. AMPK and PPAR-\u947? are not minor footnotes in the MASLD story; they sit close to its mechanistic core. AMPK functions as the cell's energy sentinel, switching on catabolic, fat-burning programs and switching off the lipogenic ones whenever the energy charge of the cell runs low (Hardie, 2011; Garcia & Shaw, 2017). PPAR-\u947?, conversely, is the master regulator of adipogenesis and a key driver of lipid storage in hepatocytes when it is inappropriately active outside adipose tissue (Tontonoz & Spiegelman, 2008). A compound, or a mixture of compounds, capable of nudging both dials in the right direction at once is not a trivial finding \u8212? it is a plausible answer to one half of the puzzle that makes MASLD so difficult to treat with a single drug.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The disease itself needs little introduction to anyone working in a Nigerian or West African clinical setting, though the label keeps changing. What clinicians called non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) for three decades was renamed MASLD in 2023 by international consensus, a shift intended to anchor the diagnosis explicitly to metabolic dysfunction \u8212? obesity, insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia \u8212? rather than to the absence of alcohol use, and to remove the stigmatising connotations of the old name (Rinella et al., 2023; Eslam et al., 2020). Whatever it is called, it is common: global prevalence estimates cluster around a quarter to a third of all adults, climbing substantially higher among people who are obese or who have type 2 diabetes (Younossi et al., 2016, 2018). Left to progress, it carries real risk of advancing to steatohepatitis, fibrosis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma, while independently raising cardiovascular risk along the way (Friedman, Neuschwander-Tetri, Rinella, & Sanyal, 2018). And the economic burden it imposes \u8212? on health systems, on productivity, on the patients themselves \u8212? is neither small nor evenly distributed; it falls hardest on precisely the low- and middle-income settings least equipped to absorb it (Paik et al., 2020).\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Pharmacology has not stood still. The arrival of GLP-1 receptor agonists such as semaglutide, shown in a landmark 2021 trial to produce meaningful histological improvement in non-alcoholic steatohepatitis, marked a genuine breakthrough (Newsome et al., 2021). But breakthroughs of this kind tend to be expensive, require sustained injectable administration, and are simply not available at scale to the populations where MASLD's burden is rising fastest \u8212? including much of sub-Saharan Africa, where nutrition transition, urbanisation, and rising obesity rates are colliding to produce exactly the metabolic conditions the disease thrives on (Friedman et al., 2018). It is this gap \u8212? between a disease that is common, serious, and rapidly growing in Africa, and a pharmacological armamentarium that is largely priced and engineered for wealthier health systems \u8212? that gives the search for affordable, multi-target, plant-derived alternatives its urgency.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 African and South Asian traditional medicine systems have never approached metabolic and hepatic disease with single-compound logic. Where Western pharmacology has historically prized the isolation of one active molecule against one molecular target, traditional polyherbal formulations routinely combine several plants on the working assumption \u8212? long predating any molecular biology \u8212? that complaints with many causes respond best to remedies with many simultaneous actions (Sofowora, 1993; Iwu, 1993; Gurib-Fakim, 2006). Contemporary network pharmacology has, in its own vocabulary, arrived at much the same conclusion: complex diseases driven by multiple interacting pathways are often better served by multi-target interventions than by maximally selective single-target drugs, and well-designed combinations of natural products can produce additive or synergistic effects beyond what any one component delivers alone (Hopkins, 2008; Wagner & Ulrich-Merzenich, 2009). The quadriherbal logic pursued in this article, in other words, is not a novelty invented for this paper; it is an old idea from African and Asian ethnomedicine, re-examined with the tools and the mechanistic vocabulary of modern molecular pharmacology.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Running in parallel to the bamboo findings is a separate, independently developed literature on {\i Mangifera indica} \u8212? the common mango \u8212? whose leaves, rich in the xanthone mangiferin alongside C-glycosylflavones and phenolic acids, have repeatedly demonstrated antidiabetic, antioxidant, and hepatoprotective activity in published studies. Read next to the bamboo data, the complementarity is almost too clean: where {\i B. vulgaris} works the AMPK/PPAR-\u947? axis governing lipid handling, {\i M. indica} works the incretin axis governing glucose handling and antioxidant defence. Two plants, two largely non-overlapping mechanisms, one disease.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 And yet, so far as a careful search of the published literature could establish, nobody had written this combination down. This preliminary investigation exists to close that specific, narrow gap \u8212? not by running new experiments, but by doing the synthesis work that the existing published evidence already supports, and by being explicit, at every step, about what that synthesis can and cannot claim.\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Four tasks organise what follows. First, the published findings on {\i B. vulgaris} and the supporting {\i M. indica} literature are critically synthesised side by side. Second, the molecular pathways each plant engages are mapped against one another. Third, a rational, standardized quadriherbal formulation (QHF) is proposed. Fourth, the entire exercise is framed throughout as preliminary and hypothesis-generating, with a concrete, staged roadmap for future research.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 2. Conceptual and Mechanistic Background\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 2.1 MASLD: From a Diagnosis of Exclusion to a Metabolic Disease in Its Own Right\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 For most of its clinical history, fatty liver disease was defined negatively. The 2023 multisociety consensus statement replaced that exclusionary logic with a positive, metabolic one (Rinella et al., 2023; Eslam et al., 2020). Pathophysiologically, the disease is explained through a \u8220?multiple-hit\u8221? model: insulin resistance and excess caloric intake drive hepatic lipid accumulation, and lipotoxicity, oxidative stress, gut-derived endotoxin, and genetic susceptibility then determine progression (Day & James, 1998; Friedman et al., 2018).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 2.2 AMPK as the Cell's Energy Sentinel\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 AMP-activated protein kinase functions as a cellular fuel gauge. Once activated, it suppresses de novo lipogenesis, promotes fatty acid oxidation, and inhibits SREBP-1c (Hardie, 2011; Garcia & Shaw, 2017) \u8212? the same lever metformin is understood to pull (Sarkar & Das, 2026).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 2.3 PPAR-\u947?, Adipocyte Biology, and the Liver's Misplaced Fat Storage\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 PPAR-\u947? is the master regulator of adipocyte differentiation and the target of thiazolidinedione drugs (Tontonoz & Spiegelman, 2008). Hepatic PPAR-\u947? becomes upregulated in steatotic liver, driving ectopic lipid storage; downregulating it removes one proximate driver of steatosis (Sarkar & Das, 2026).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 2.4 The Incretin Axis: Where Mangifera indica Enters the Picture\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 GLP-1 augments insulin secretion, slows gastric emptying, and promotes satiety \u8212? the axis exploited by semaglutide (Newsome et al., 2021). Mango leaf extracts stimulate this pathway via MAPK/Wnt signalling (Jariyapongskul et al., 2025; Nadella et al., 2016; Muruganandan et al., 2005).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 2.5 Polyherbalism, Synergy, and African Ethnopharmacological Traditions\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 African ethnopharmacological compendia document extensive traditional polyherbal use for metabolic and hepatic complaints (Sofowora, 1993; Iwu, 1993; Gurib-Fakim, 2006). Modern synergy and network pharmacology research reaches compatible conclusions about multi-target combinations (Wagner & Ulrich-Merzenich, 2009; Hopkins, 2008).\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 3. Methods: A Literature Synthesis, Not a New Experiment\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 No animals were dosed, no human participants were recruited, and no new laboratory assay of any kind was run. Every mechanistic claim originates in already-published, peer-reviewed literature, public phytochemical databases, or established ethnopharmacological reviews.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 3.1 Literature Search Strategy and Data Sources\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The synthesis drew on three layers of evidence: the 2026 Sarkar and Das study; published {\i M. indica} pharmacology; and African ethnopharmacological reviews used to identify candidates for two further formulation positions. No formal PRISMA flow diagram was generated, as this was a targeted rather than exhaustive systematic review.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 3.2 Data Extraction and Mechanistic Mapping\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Animal model details, dosing regimens, biochemical/histological/molecular outcomes, and phytochemical profiles were extracted and cross-referenced to identify genuine complementarity rather than redundant activity.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 3.3 Rationale Development for the Quadriherbal Formulation\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 {\i B. vulgaris} was retained as the core component; {\i M. indica} was included for complementary incretin effects; two further African plants were sought for overlapping bioactives and traditional metabolic evidence without known toxicity risk. No in vitro synergy testing or animal dosing of the combination was conducted; proposed ratios are explicitly hypothetical.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 3.4 Phytochemical and In Silico Considerations\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Public databases (PubChem, ChEMBL) were consulted for known bioactivities of mangiferin, isoorientin, and vitexin to support a conceptual compound-target-pathway map. No new docking simulations were run.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 3.5 Limitations of the Secondary Approach\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 The synthesis rests on published, mostly single-plant data; publication bias is an endemic risk; no human or toxicological data exist for the combination; and Herb 3/Herb 4 identities remain provisional.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 4. Results: Synthesis of Evidence and the Proposed Formulation\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.1 What Sarkar and Das (2026) Actually Found\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 In HFD-induced obese, MASLD-modelled rats, daily oral {\i B. vulgaris} leaf extract (200\u8211?400 mg/kg, 4 weeks) significantly attenuated body weight gain, improved adipocyte function and liver histology, increased phosphorylated AMPK, and decreased PPAR-\u947? expression in liver and adipose tissue. In silico data supported interactions between isoorientin/vitexin and relevant metabolic targets (Sarkar & Das, 2026).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.2 The Complementary Case for Mangifera indica\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Mango leaf extracts and mangiferin stimulate GLP-1 secretion, improve glycaemic control and HOMA-IR, and show antioxidant/hepatoprotective activity in toxin-induced liver injury models (Jariyapongskul et al., 2025; Nadella et al., 2016; Muruganandan et al., 2005).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.3 Candidates for the Remaining Two Slots: Herb 3 and Herb 4\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 African ethnopharmacological compendia point toward plausible candidates: {\i Moringa oleifera}, {\i Vernonia amygdalina} (bitter leaf), {\i Gongronema latifolium}, and {\i Hibiscus sabdariffa} (zobo) (Sofowora, 1993; Iwu, 1993; Gurib-Fakim, 2006). None of these candidacies is settled; each requires dedicated phytochemical and mechanistic scrutiny.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.4 Proposed Quadriherbal Formulation (QHF): Composition and Rationale\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 A standardized formulation is proposed as an illustrative starting point: 40% {\i B. vulgaris} leaf extract; 30% {\i M. indica} leaf extract; 15% Herb 3; 15% Herb 4.\par
