\pard\qc\fs18 Proportions and herb identities are illustrative and hypothesis-generating only; none of the four-component combinations has been tested empirically.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.5 Predicted Multi-Target Mechanism\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 AMPK activation promotes fatty acid oxidation and suppresses lipogenesis; PPAR-\u947? downregulation limits hepatic lipid storage; GLP-1 secretion improves insulin sensitivity; and the full phytochemical matrix is predicted to reduce oxidative stress and metaflammation (Wagner & Ulrich-Merzenich, 2009).\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 4.6 Endpoints Proposed for Future Primary Validation\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Future studies should evaluate liver enzymes and histological scoring; glycaemic parameters and lipid profile; molecular markers (p-AMPK/AMPK, PPAR-\u947?, ACC, SREBP-1c, CPT-1); oxidative stress and inflammatory markers; and dose-response/safety data against single extracts and positive controls.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 5. Discussion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.1 What a Secondary Synthesis Is Good For\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 Anchoring this proposal in a single, robust, mechanistically detailed study, and adding an independently supported second mechanism, aligns with both network pharmacology (Hopkins, 2008) and African traditional polyherbal practice (Sofowora, 1993; Iwu, 1993), offering an efficient, low-cost, ethically sound first step before new experiments.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.2 The Mechanistic Logic, Restated Plainly\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 AMPK activation addresses fat-burning; PPAR-\u947? downregulation addresses fat storage; GLP-1 enhancement addresses glucose handling; antioxidant/anti-inflammatory load addresses secondary damage (Day & James, 1998; Friedman et al., 2018). Whether the combination behaves this way in vivo remains untested.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.3 Where the Evidence Runs Out: Limitations\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 No direct evidence exists for the four-component combination; source studies are short-term rodent models; the translational gap to heterogeneous human MASLD is real (Friedman et al., 2018); ratios are conceptual; publication bias is a risk; no human or toxicological data exist; Herb 3/4 identities remain provisional.\par
\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.4 A Staged Roadmap Toward Clinical Translation\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 In vitro mechanistic confirmation; optimised animal dose-response and comparative studies; standardization and quality control; acute/sub-chronic toxicity studies (OECD guidelines); exploration of emerging pathways; and, finally, a proof-of-concept clinical study under appropriate ethical oversight.\par
