\pard\qj\b\i\fs24\sb200\sa100 5.5 Why This Matters for Nigeria and the Wider Region\b0\i0\par
\pard\qj\sa160 MASLD and type 2 diabetes are rising sharply across sub-Saharan Africa while newer pharmacological options remain largely out of reach (Paik et al., 2020; Friedman et al., 2018). A validated quadriherbal formulation would offer a standardized phytomedicine compatible with functional-food, nutraceutical, or community-health delivery models suited to resource-constrained settings.\par
\pard\qj\b\fs28\sb240\sa120 6. Conclusion\b0\fs24\par
\pard\qj\sa160 This preliminary investigation sets out a coherent mechanistic and ethnopharmacological rationale for a novel quadriherbal formulation aimed at MASLD and associated metabolic dysfunction, by reading the 2026 {\i Bambusa vulgaris} findings alongside complementary {\i Mangifera indica} evidence and African ethnopharmacological candidates for two further herbs. It makes no efficacy claim, and requires validation through properly designed primary research beginning with in vitro confirmation and ending, if all goes well, with a clinical study. Primary empirical data on the proposed QHF are not yet available, and they are needed.\par
